WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:04.140
Like a splash of cold water in the face, Aquaman
has arrived.

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:08.360
And though it's great, it's also a little
confusing, jammed with a century's worth of

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:09.620
undersea exposition.

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:13.679
Don't worry, though, we're here to help you
keep track of all those lost tribes and strange

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:14.320
artifacts.

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:18.120
So grab your life preserver, because here's
a spoiler-filled explanation of the ending

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:18.920
of Aquaman.

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:23.120
Arthur and Mera's search for the legendary
lost trident of King Atlan took them to some

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:27.620
exotic locales, but none were more unexpected
than the center of the Earth itself.

00:00:28.269 --> 00:00:31.940
But longtime comic book fans know that if
there's one place you should always expect

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:34.580
the unexpected, it's the land of Skartaris.

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:40.819
See, in the 1970s DC Comics series The Warlord,
it was revealed that Skartaris is a prehistoric

00:00:40.819 --> 00:00:44.909
jungle world accessible through portals that
connect it to Earth, and after the destruction

00:00:44.909 --> 00:00:49.530
of Atlantis, a band of refugees traveled through
one of these portals to Skartaris and founded

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:50.460
New Atlantis.

00:00:50.769 --> 00:00:54.219
Though Aquaman didn't run into any of the
citizens of New Atlantis during his brief

00:00:54.219 --> 00:00:58.379
time on the other side of the portal, it would
make sense if the final resting place of Atlan's

00:00:58.379 --> 00:01:03.389
trident was actually Skartaris, as it would
explain the fate of the "missing tribe" which

00:01:03.389 --> 00:01:06.300
opens the door to a whole new world of adventure,
quite literally.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:11.560
Once Arthur finally got his hands on Atlan's
trident, fans got to see an incredibly confusing

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:16.040
sequence where it seemingly lit up the nervous
systems of a bunch of random sea creatures.

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:20.299
There was no concrete explanation as to what
was actually happening, leaving viewers to

00:01:20.299 --> 00:01:25.189
wonder just what exactly this mythical rident
actually does other than spark up what appeared

00:01:25.189 --> 00:01:26.940
to be a bonus level from Tetris Effect.

00:01:27.409 --> 00:01:31.479
Earlier in the film, it's said that the trident
gave Atlan command of the seas.

00:01:31.479 --> 00:01:35.729
The prevailing theory is that the trident
essentially enhanced Aquaman's natural ability

00:01:35.729 --> 00:01:39.799
to communicate with sea-life, amplifying it
to the point where he could exert his will

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:41.280
and actually control it.

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:45.499
That would explain how he was able to command
the karathen, as well as the shark mounts

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:47.060
used by King Orm's forces.

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:51.580
It even seemed to extend to the trench-dwelling
Atlantean descendants who had devolved back

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:52.880
into a more monstrous form.

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:54.940
You have to hand it to Princess Mera.

00:01:55.280 --> 00:02:00.040
Not only did she save Atlantis, and the surface
world as well, she also managed to free herself

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:04.760
from an unwanted political marriage by replacing
the ambitious and scheming Prince Orm with

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:08.460
the much more suggestible and, well, kinda
dumber Arthur.

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:11.810
It was a brilliant maneuver from the real
power behind the throne.

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:15.200
"Let's just say, you do your best thinking
when you're not thinking at all."

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:17.140
"That was the worst pep-talk.

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:18.220
Ever."

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:22.940
And Arthur is going to need Mera's political
acumen, because he is way out of his depth,

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:24.100
no pun intended.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:27.380
So far, it doesn't seem like he's even heard
the word "diplomacy."

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:32.620
Sure, a lot was made of Aquaman's unique ability
to bring the surface world and Atlantis together.

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:36.290
But you have to be able to do more than talk
to fish to get that done, you have to talk

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:36.780
to people.

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:39.460
And so far, that doesn't really seem to be
his strong suit.

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:44.480
Did anybody really think Black Manta was dead
just because his head exploded and he fell

00:02:44.489 --> 00:02:49.060
hundreds of feet into the angry ocean, bouncing
off every single outcropping of rock in Italy

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:49.840
on the way down?

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:50.600
Of course not!

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:54.879
Considering the lengths to which director
James Wan went to in order to shoehorn Manta

00:02:54.879 --> 00:02:58.870
and his origin story into a film that didn't
need him, there's no way he was going to be

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:00.129
written out that easily.

00:03:00.129 --> 00:03:03.320
So what can fans expect from Manta in future
Aquaman films?

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:07.200
Well, comic book readers have a pretty good
idea, and it ain't pretty.

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:12.269
Way back in the 1970s, Black Manta was at
the center of one of the most shocking storylines

00:03:12.269 --> 00:03:17.269
in comics history, as he finally got his revenge
on Aquaman by murdering Arthur and Mera's

00:03:17.269 --> 00:03:18.040
infant son.

00:03:18.489 --> 00:03:23.260
We're not sure a sequel will get quite that
dark, but Aquaman's dad is still alive, and

00:03:23.271 --> 00:03:27.380
would seem like a ripe target for Manta's
vengeance… especially since the mid-credits

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:31.340
scene showed Manta teamed up with Atlantis-obsessed
researcher Stephen Shin.

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:35.900
In the comics, Shin was a friend of Aquaman's
dad who betrayed them both to satisfy his

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:38.180
compulsion to find the location of Atlantis.

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:40.620
So will things play out similarly in the movies?

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:45.159
With Black Manta and Dr. Shin working together,
not to mention the continued danger of Prince

00:03:45.159 --> 00:03:49.909
Orm and the existence of Skartaris, one thing
is for sure: Aquaman 2 will have no shortage

00:03:49.909 --> 00:03:51.240
of plotlines to choose from.

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:55.659
Besides giving him the ability to command
the creatures of the sea, the trident of Atlan

00:03:55.659 --> 00:04:00.250
was also supposed to serve as the ultimate
trump card, granting Arthur the title of King

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:00.740
of Atlantis.

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:05.480
Yet, when he showed up with the trident to
try and stop the Atlantean civil war, surprisingly

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:10.140
few people on either side of the battle seemed
to actually notice or care, at least at first.

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:14.280
It's clear that his goal of uniting the seven
tribes of Atlantis is going to take a whole

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:17.920
lot more work than just raising aloft the
underwater equivalent of Excalibur.

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:19.540
As a wise man once said:

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:25.900
"Supreme executive power derives from a mandate
from the masses, not from some farcical aquatic

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:26.600
ceremony."

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:31.720
Becoming king isn't the end of Arthur's work:
it's just the beginning, magic trident or

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:32.680
no magic trident.

